WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.335
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:03.335 --> 00:00:04.437
who you know from her work

00:00:04.437 --> 00:00:05.772
on "Grey's Anatomy"
and "Killing Eve."

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:08.674
She stars in the new
Disney/Pixar film "Turning Red,"

00:00:08.674 --> 00:00:11.344
which is available on Disney+
starting this Friday.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.645
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.647
-Aah!
-[ Body thuds ]

00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:15.781
-Oh.

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:17.850
-[ Breathing heavily ]

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.720
This isn't happening!
This isn't happening!

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:23.489
[ Whimpering ]

00:00:23.489 --> 00:00:26.192
[ Sniffs ]

00:00:26.192 --> 00:00:27.827
[ Retches ]

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.095
[ Whimpering ]

00:00:29.095 --> 00:00:30.296
Wake up, wake up, wake up!

00:00:30.296 --> 00:00:31.430
-Mei Mei!
-[ Gasps ]

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.165
-Is everything okay?
-Don't come in here!

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:36.368
-Honey. What's going on, honey?
Are you sick?

00:00:36.368 --> 00:00:39.171
Is it a fever, a stomach ache,
chills, constipation?

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:40.506
-No!

00:00:40.506 --> 00:00:43.476
-Wait. Is it...that?

00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:47.513
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Sandra Oh, everybody!

00:00:47.513 --> 00:00:49.182
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:55.721
♪♪

00:00:55.721 --> 00:01:02.294
♪♪

00:01:02.294 --> 00:01:04.096
-People!
-Isn't it lovely?

00:01:04.096 --> 00:01:06.131
-People!
-People!

00:01:06.131 --> 00:01:08.134
-People!
-[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:08.134 --> 00:01:11.971
-Because, last time, we spoke
middle of pandemic.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.473
-Mid-pandemic.

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:14.741
-You were at
your sister's house.

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.008
-I was quarantined
for two weeks.

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:17.543
-That's right.
You were all alone.

00:01:17.543 --> 00:01:18.811
-I was all alone,

00:01:18.811 --> 00:01:20.580
quarantining in North Vancouver
at my sister's.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:23.249
-Yep.
-And I think, like all of us,

00:01:23.249 --> 00:01:26.486
you were a little stir-crazy,
had cabin fever.

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.588
And most of the interview is you
running around with the laptop,

00:01:29.588 --> 00:01:31.090
showing me your sister's house.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.326
-Yes, yes.
-How did she feel about that?

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.261
-She wasn't pleased.
-Yeah.

00:01:35.261 --> 00:01:37.830
-She wasn't pleased.
But it's a bit of reality.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.632
You know what I mean?
'Cause it's, like, beside the --

00:01:40.632 --> 00:01:43.469
You know, those cat things
that it's like a little --

00:01:43.469 --> 00:01:45.171
It's a carpet thing?
-Yes.

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:49.075
-Right? The ping-pong table

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.910
that's beside the piano
in the living room.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.645
-Yeah. It was -- Right.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:55.081
I should note,
it wasn't a case of you saying,

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:56.682
"Let me show you
the beautiful decor."

00:01:56.682 --> 00:01:59.685
It was more like you were like,
"This place is crazy."

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:01.821
So I think your sister
has every right.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.689
I mean, here she is, housing you
through a quarantine,

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:05.191
and yet, there you are,
shaming her house

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:06.692
on national television.
-No, no, no, no.

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:10.063
It's actually more of just being
that perfect little sister.

00:02:10.063 --> 00:02:12.398
-Oh. Interesting.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:12.398 --> 00:02:14.234
-Oh, so, you wanted
to bust her on this?

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:15.735
-She's, like,
a serious lawyer lady.

00:02:15.735 --> 00:02:17.170
-Okay, gotcha.
-Right?

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.271
You got to put a little bit of,
like, you know, fun in it,

00:02:19.271 --> 00:02:20.940
and I think that
I've always provided that

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.842
for my family, shamelessly.

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.410
-Shamelessly?
-Shamelessly.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.412
-It must be fun to be making --

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.047
I mean, obviously,
we talk about family.

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:30.716
This is -- Disney/Pixar
always makes family films,

00:02:30.716 --> 00:02:32.317
but this is one about a family.

00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:36.222
You play a mother who has
very high hopes for a daughter

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.657
who is going through --
like a lot of --

00:02:38.657 --> 00:02:41.460
like every kid, like, these
changes of getting older.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.062
And it just seems like
a wonderful film

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:44.530
to have been a part of.
-It is.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.767
You know, our director, Domee,
she won an Oscar

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.268
for a short film, "Bao."

00:02:49.268 --> 00:02:51.570
She describes it
as "magical puberty."

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:54.474
-Yeah.
-So, when my child, right --

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:56.909
So, it's basically, you know --
"Turning Red" is centered

00:02:56.909 --> 00:02:59.312
on a 13-year-old
Chinese Canadian girl

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.614
who's going through puberty
and her relationship

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.549
with her mom,
which needs to change.

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.951
But the metaphor
of the red panda

00:03:05.951 --> 00:03:08.320
is, like, the messiness
of puberty and changing.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:10.890
You see. She's like,
"What is this body?

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:12.491
What is this smell?"

00:03:12.491 --> 00:03:14.627
And it's so great to see,
you know,

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:16.829
on any animated --
or any picture.

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:18.998
But like, you know,
a Pixar picture that explores

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.635
unabashedly also
the inner life of a girl,

00:03:22.635 --> 00:03:24.336
where it's like,
"I don't like myself.

00:03:24.336 --> 00:03:25.972
What's going on?
What are these emotions?"

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:27.406
That's really
what it's about, yeah.

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:28.941
-That's a really cool thing
to work on.

00:03:28.941 --> 00:03:32.845
-I related so much to the
13-year-old character of Meilin.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:36.148
But for me, I was like --
You know,

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:40.320
I was just really emotional
and hyperactive.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.287
-Did your family know
what to do with you?

00:03:42.287 --> 00:03:44.924
-No, but the thing is,
it's, like, no matter what --

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.258
You know, as a parent --

00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:48.627
Obviously I play Ming,
the parent.

00:03:48.627 --> 00:03:50.796
I don't think that you're ever
going to understand

00:03:50.796 --> 00:03:53.600
or be prepared for
when the child changes

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.601
and turns into, like,
a hairy beast.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.002
-Yeah.
-And then he's not doing

00:03:57.002 --> 00:03:58.237
what you want, you know?

00:03:58.237 --> 00:03:59.671
And the reverse, obviously.

00:03:59.671 --> 00:04:02.975
It's like, you know --
It's really focusing on

00:04:02.975 --> 00:04:04.310
a young person's point of view,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:06.879
who's just so confused
about this time, you know?

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.915
So, the "turning red,"
there's one part of it

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:11.050
I really feel, like,
so proud of,

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:13.986
is that it gives some
sort of language for kids

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:17.056
and for adults to talk about
this time called puberty,

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:18.524
which is gonna come.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.860
A lot of emotions.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.828
And, "You're gonna probably
disappoint me.

00:04:22.828 --> 00:04:24.496
I'm probably gonna
disappoint you."

00:04:24.496 --> 00:04:27.433
-That's very helpful.
A helpful thing to go through.

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:32.071
When we first met, it was when
you were here doing "SNL."

00:04:32.071 --> 00:04:33.572
But before you had hosted "SNL,"

00:04:33.572 --> 00:04:36.676
you hosted the Golden Globes
with my friend Andy Samberg,

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.777
who is supposed to go
through puberty any day.

00:04:38.777 --> 00:04:40.146
-Yeah, finally.
We're waiting.

00:04:40.146 --> 00:04:41.547
-We're waiting.
-We're waiting.

00:04:41.547 --> 00:04:43.683
-I thought it would maybe
happen that Globes week.

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:45.618
[ Laughter ]
-It was very stressful.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:48.420
-But it was a thrilling
combination at the time.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.456
Do you have fond memories
of getting up there with him?

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.491
-Well, he has such
great leadership, honestly.

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.593
-He really does.
-Yeah, he has great leadership.

00:04:54.593 --> 00:04:58.964
And what I was so unfamiliar
with is how you people work,

00:04:58.964 --> 00:05:01.500
how you "SNL" people work.
It's crazy.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.936
It's just crazy,
and it's insane.

00:05:03.936 --> 00:05:05.304
But it's very, very thrilling.

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:06.839
I thought it was
really, really challenging.

00:05:06.839 --> 00:05:10.610
So, you know, the way that he
brought so many of the writers

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:12.144
that are here on your show.

00:05:12.144 --> 00:05:15.080
-A lot of our writers
came out for that week.

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:16.215
They had a great time.

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:18.551
-And the way
that you basically do it --

00:05:18.551 --> 00:05:20.486
So, basically,
I'm the type of actor

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.522
who really, really
would prefer a script.

00:05:22.522 --> 00:05:24.223
-Yep.
-And so it was something

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:26.826
that was slid
under the hotel door

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:30.196
on the Saturday night
before the Sunday Globes.

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:32.097
And so I was terrified.

00:05:32.097 --> 00:05:33.933
But it was really fun.

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:36.068
-Andy has often complained
to me that there was what

00:05:36.068 --> 00:05:38.504
he believed was an A-plus bit
in the Golden Globes.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:39.838
-Oh, yeah.

00:05:39.838 --> 00:05:43.041
-This was the year that
Bradley Cooper and Lady Gaga

00:05:43.041 --> 00:05:44.443
were in "A Star is Born."

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:46.979
And there was a scene in that
movie where Jackson Maine,

00:05:46.979 --> 00:05:50.683
Bradley Cooper's character,
urinates onstage at the Grammys.

00:05:50.683 --> 00:05:53.019
-Yes.
-And Andy wanted to do that

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:54.354
at the Globes.
-Yes.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:57.356
Yes, I wanted to do that, too.
-Yeah.

00:05:57.356 --> 00:06:00.993
-It was gonna be, like -- They
wrote this serious monologue.

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:03.195
I was gonna be talking
with a serious monologue.

00:06:03.195 --> 00:06:06.432
And then Andy was -- He had
the costume and everything.

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:10.369
But it's actually really hard
to kind of, like, show pee.

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:11.771
-It's harder
than you think, yeah.

00:06:11.771 --> 00:06:13.039
-It's harder than you think.

00:06:13.039 --> 00:06:14.307
-There's a certain
kind of khaki

00:06:14.307 --> 00:06:16.008
that shows it off the best.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.778
And I know that
from, uh, middle school.

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.379
[ Laughter ]

00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:23.716
There was a time
where I was turning red.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:26.852
Yeah, but, like,
there was an actual rig.

00:06:26.852 --> 00:06:28.654
I should note that he wasn't
physically doing it.

00:06:28.654 --> 00:06:30.723
-No, we went all the way
to, like, rehearsal.

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:32.525
Again, I think, like
"Saturday Night Live,"

00:06:32.525 --> 00:06:34.893
you will do bits and skits that,

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:36.762
on the day,
they'll decide to cut.

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:39.132
-My favorite thing about
my friendship with Andy is,

00:06:39.132 --> 00:06:40.766
he'll call you really depressed.

00:06:40.766 --> 00:06:42.568
And then when you find out
the reason, it's the best.

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.336
You know, he's like,
"I'm super bummed."

00:06:44.336 --> 00:06:45.771
I'm like, "Oh, what happened?"

00:06:45.771 --> 00:06:47.139
He's like, "Aw, they won't
let me do this pee bit

00:06:47.139 --> 00:06:48.941
at the Golden Globes."

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:50.776
I'm like,
"I think you're gonna be okay."

00:06:50.776 --> 00:06:52.578
Oh, by the way,
can I ask one question?

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.746
'Cause we both know him.

00:06:53.746 --> 00:06:55.581
He thinks my dog is ugly
and looks like a rat.

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:56.983
-Oh, okay.

00:06:56.983 --> 00:06:58.451
-Can I show you
a picture of my dog?

00:06:58.451 --> 00:07:01.587
Alright. And you either say
"Andy" if you agree with Andy

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:03.189
or say "Seth"
if you agree with me.

00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:05.191
You've never seen my dog.
-I've never seen your dog.

00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:07.293
-Ready?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.330
Like a rat?
-Yeah.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.799
This is already taking too long.

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:15.768
-Seth.
-Thank you very much.

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:18.170
That's a real --
-I'm focusing on the body.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.339
The body does not look
like a rat.

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:21.807
-What about the face?
-The head does.

00:07:21.807 --> 00:07:24.610
-OK. We're gonna be right back.
We have more to talk about.

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.712
I'll be right back
with Sandra Oh.

